nd $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186404617 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404617 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ca8a0614-8f54-566a-a177-8dffc8e8215a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404618 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186404618 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404618 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "214fedb5-e346-584d-9ffc-111bf69f0cb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404619 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404619 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404619 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404619 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186404619 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46cbbf2d-cd23-5aab-9cf1-375dd44b0200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404619 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186404620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404620 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb