592186492008 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{5}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42d1d2ed-a4fb-5a99-995b-31b25dd2611a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492008 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71c26421-0b8a-5150-b5ca-ed9ed8ecdd75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492025 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ Computing $A-(-6)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8da40f3d-5d18-5923-96f9-268309e6cbba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492035 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1045 "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186519655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "64fc3270-931c-55b0-ab11-941f63cb1739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518993 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518993 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518993 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03bfb243-3f51-50bb-a40d-8bd7b816d5c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519042 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{ali